1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,019
This is Liam, in for Bear. Claude Code is
an agentic harness. Everyone says it;

2
00:00:04,019 --> 00:00:08,190
nobody shows it. So here's the model with
the harness turned off — no tools, none.

3
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:10,275
One ask: make a file with one line in it.

4
00:00:10,275 --> 00:00:14,548
It says: creating the file now. And then,
nothing. List the folder: the README I put

5
00:00:14,548 --> 00:00:18,516
there. No file. That sentence was the
whole event. A model on its own can only

6
00:00:18,516 --> 00:00:18,770
talk.

7
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:22,870
Here's the idea of this film. Claude Code
is not a better model. It's the same model

8
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:26,823
you talk to in the chat window, wrapped in
something: a list of what it may touch,

9
00:00:26,823 --> 00:00:30,680
rules about touching it, and a loop that
keeps asking what next. That wrapper is

10
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:34,633
the harness. I'm going to switch it off,
half on, and on, so you can see the model

11
00:00:34,633 --> 00:00:37,670
without it — and then build a tiny harness
of my own around it.

12
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:39,659
Five words before we start. Headless:

13
00:00:39,659 --> 00:00:43,956
Claude Code run from the shell with one
prompt — it answers and exits. Tools list:

14
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:48,357
what the model may call — read, write, run
a command, use a connector. Connector, or

15
00:00:48,357 --> 00:00:52,549
MCP: an outside service plugged in as
tools, like Google Drive. Permission mode:

16
00:00:52,549 --> 00:00:56,741
the rule for which tool calls need your
yes. Stream json: the whole session as a

17
00:00:56,741 --> 00:00:58,680
log, one event per line. The receipt.

18
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:02,300
Turn the harness on and ask for the
receipt — stream json. The first event

19
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:05,871
isn't the model. It's the harness
introducing itself. A permission mode. A

20
00:01:05,871 --> 00:01:09,779
tools list: thirty built in, and a hundred
and forty more from connectors on this

21
00:01:09,779 --> 00:01:13,736
Mac. Five MCP servers. Forty skills. Nine
agents. The folder it's fenced to. Where

22
00:01:13,736 --> 00:01:17,451
its memory lives. None of that is the
model. All of it is what the model gets

23
00:01:17,451 --> 00:01:20,780
handed before it reads my sentence. That's
the harness, in one event.

24
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:24,926
Same model. Same sentence. Now Write is on
the list. One tool call, one file, two

25
00:01:24,926 --> 00:01:28,517
turns. Cat it: hello. The difference
between run one and run two is not

26
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:32,765
intelligence. It's a list. That is what a
harness is: the list of what the model can

27
00:01:32,765 --> 00:01:36,913
touch, the rules for touching it, and the
loop that keeps asking 'what next' until

28
00:01:36,913 --> 00:01:37,570
it says done.

29
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:41,593
Here's the run I didn't plan. Built-in
tools off — but the connectors on this Mac

30
00:01:41,593 --> 00:01:45,321
were still attached. So with no way to
write to disk, it reached for the one

31
00:01:45,321 --> 00:01:49,245
write tool it had: my Google Drive. And
the permission layer stopped it. Then it

32
00:01:49,245 --> 00:01:52,776
tried Write anyway — not enabled. Two
lessons in one run. The model uses

33
00:01:52,776 --> 00:01:56,750
whatever tools the harness leaves on the
table. And the harness has a second job:

34
00:01:56,750 --> 00:01:57,240
saying no.

35
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:01,320
Now the part people call harness
engineering — and it's ten lines. A task

36
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:05,791
file with three boxes. A prompt: read the
file, do only the first unchecked task,

37
00:02:05,791 --> 00:02:10,208
tick it, stop. And a shell loop that runs
Claude Code three times. Each run is a

38
00:02:10,208 --> 00:02:14,900
fresh session. Fresh context, no memory of
the last one. The only memory is the file.

39
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:19,724
Iteration one. Read the file. Write a dot
md. Edit one box. One line back: did task,

40
00:02:19,724 --> 00:02:24,327
write a.md. It didn't do b, it didn't do c
— the prompt said only the first. Four

41
00:02:24,327 --> 00:02:28,988
turns, done, session over. Iteration two
starts from zero and reads the file again

42
00:02:28,988 --> 00:02:30,920
— and the file now says a is done.

43
00:02:30,970 --> 00:02:35,180
Check it. Three boxes ticked. Three files,
right contents. The diff: three lines

44
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:37,233
changed in the task file, nothing else.

45
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:41,601
And the receipt for 'fresh context': six
session files on disk — one per run, three

46
00:02:41,601 --> 00:02:45,706
for the loop. No iteration ever saw
another's transcript. The loop didn't make

47
00:02:45,706 --> 00:02:49,390
the model smarter. It made the job small
enough to finish, every time.

48
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:53,323
So here's the picture, from the inside
out. The model: it can only talk. Around

49
00:02:53,323 --> 00:02:57,157
it, the prompt — the system prompt, your
CLAUDE.md. That's prompt engineering.

50
00:02:57,157 --> 00:03:00,942
Around that, context: the tools, the
files, the memory — context engineering;

51
00:03:00,942 --> 00:03:04,825
that's the tools list you saw in the
manifest. Around that, the harness proper:

52
00:03:04,825 --> 00:03:08,413
the loop that asks what next, the
permission layer that said no to Google

53
00:03:08,413 --> 00:03:12,346
Drive, the fence around the folder, a turn
limit. That's Claude Code. And around

54
00:03:12,346 --> 00:03:16,426
that, your loop — the shell for, the task
file. Harness engineering is choosing the

55
00:03:16,426 --> 00:03:18,490
outer rings so the inner ones can't drift.

56
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:22,276
Who did what. Step one, mine: one ask,
three harness settings — that's the

57
00:03:22,276 --> 00:03:26,063
experiment. Tools off, Claude said
creating the file now; the handoff was a

58
00:03:26,063 --> 00:03:29,950
file on disk, and it missed it. Step
three, the dangerous middle: a confident

59
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:34,040
sentence with no tool call under it, and
only reading the stream caught it. Tools

60
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:38,028
on: one Write, handoff met — cat says
hello. Then I wrote the task file and the

61
00:03:38,028 --> 00:03:41,865
loop — tool orchestration; that's the
whole design. Claude ran three passes:

62
00:03:41,865 --> 00:03:45,702
read, write, edit one box, handoff met
three times. Two zeros — interpretive

63
00:03:45,702 --> 00:03:49,640
judgment, executive integration. That's
not a gap. The loop moved the judgment

64
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:51,660
into the task file, where I can read it.

65
00:03:51,710 --> 00:03:55,845
So what was mine. I must decide the unit
of work — one task per pass. I must choose

66
00:03:55,845 --> 00:03:59,931
what's on the tools list; the model will
use whatever's there, including my Drive.

67
00:03:59,931 --> 00:04:03,967
I must check the disk, not the sentence. I
should keep the memory in a file I can

68
00:04:03,967 --> 00:04:08,002
read. Claude can do one small task per
fresh context, reliably — three for three.

69
00:04:08,002 --> 00:04:12,188
It can use any tool left on the table. It
should say when it has no tool for the job

70
00:04:12,188 --> 00:04:16,223
— it did, on the half-off run. It should
stop when the prompt says stop — it did,

71
00:04:16,223 --> 00:04:20,010
three times. The tools list is mine. The
loop is mine. The model just talks.

72
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:24,107
Let's recap with Claude. A model alone
said 'creating the file now' and created

73
00:04:24,107 --> 00:04:28,359
nothing. The same model with Write on the
list made the file in one call. Half-off,

74
00:04:28,359 --> 00:04:32,354
it reached for Google Drive and the
permission layer said no. A ten-line shell

75
00:04:32,354 --> 00:04:36,299
loop ran it three times with a fresh
context each pass, and the task file was

76
00:04:36,299 --> 00:04:40,346
the only memory: three for three. The
harness is the tools list, the rules, and

77
00:04:40,346 --> 00:04:44,546
the loop — and Claude Code is one. What
would prove this reel wrong: a run with an

78
00:04:44,546 --> 00:04:46,800
empty tools list that leaves a file on
disk.

79
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:51,138
Your turn. Open Claude Code in an empty
folder and paste this: Write tasks.md with

80
00:04:51,138 --> 00:04:55,268
three one-line tasks, and LOOP.md — a
prompt that does only the first unchecked

81
00:04:55,268 --> 00:04:59,504
task, ticks it, and stops. Then give me
the shell loop that runs you three times,

82
00:04:59,504 --> 00:05:03,948
a fresh session each pass, with only Read,
Write and Edit allowed. Run it. Then check

83
00:05:03,948 --> 00:05:05,360
